00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:05.360 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  it's a great pleasure to be here and  really a great honor to be moderating  this panel with such an incredible  inspiring group of panelists I would  like to thank all of you for sharing  your wonderful work with us and also  thank our conference organizers for  bringing within this audience all of us  here together  so what I'll try to do now is to extract  some of the questions that were coming  up through the different presentations  and then use these questions to relate  them to some of the work done here  currently at MIT  well what does it mean to be material  today in the world experiencing and  existential crisis rapid depletion of  natural resources cultural conflicts and  social inequality it is within this both  local and global discussion about the  effects of unequal distribution of  wealth cultural and social diversity in  cities as well as the corporate  governance of Technology with our  panelists bring in their imaginative and  critical insight offering new avenues  for exploration critical reflections on  society but also really inspiring forms  for creation of alternatives  socio-political realities  situated in an intersection between art  cultural science and technology the work  of our panelists is compelling on so  many levels especially in the way  technology's engaged beyond pure techn  optimism and innovation rhetoric made  we're about to uncover a new material  logic for how we can meaningfully  identify and interact with the world  Lucy desired hussein's work on migration  displacement and isolation resonates  with some of that critical art and  design legacies of MIT for example the  interrogative design methodology of the  artist christopher gage code that you  see here former director of CBS who  shape the thinking of generations here  at MIT Media Lab the artists architects  engineers hippies alien stuff that  you've seen the slides is relevant today  as a cultural mediator embracing the  digital realm to translate the messages  that are recorded in these monitors that  you see and then the physical aspect of  this stick for the contemporary migrant  is in this little tube that you see  there where you can carry your memories  or whether they are smell or a material  will you and they help you come to terms  with your new cultural environment my  own body of work on the wearable mosques  for example is informed by this type of  thinking about art and design and the  form of interrogating cultural norms and  stereotypes as they are mediated through  digital images and deconstructing these  stereotypes through material and  transferable forms we live at a time  when wearing a veil a toolbar or other  culturally signifying headgear means  that one body can be perceived as a  threat the culturally rooted paranoia  and suspiciousness represent the type of  crisis that is everywhere and affecting  all of us and tomorrow March defines the  transcultural static the time developing  here aims to provide an understanding of  cultural differences as an opportunity  for creating a dialogue and empathy  between cultures it is inspiring to find  the same conversation being force within  our own scientific community at MIT for  example in the labs of Professor poles  or Cordero whose investigations of  complex network systems such as  microbial communities bring in new ideas  of our body is a ecosystem in which not  only our bacteria has socialized but  also in bacteria has conflict and also  it is taking care of each other our  panelists have also presented us with  their multifaceted explorations of the  body central to their exploration is the  concept of the second scale  and I would like to highlight three  layers of meanings that you maybe can  comment on in the discussion the first  regard the relationship between the self  the culture and Technology Media labs  researchers in Dakar for example is  exploring this relationship by creating  cultural technologies that hybridize the  materiality of the skin with the digital  technologies in service of self  expression and communication  the second dimension relates to the  sensing properties of the skin sensors  that have grown rather than manufactured  as in the case of the tangible media  biologic project that think the  biological and engineering approaches  using ancient of tyria that are known  from the Japanese not - preparation to  create a synthetic skin that extends the  idea of the skin both functionally and  symbolically we might then ask our  panelists here what does this merging of  the digital and material on the body  mean for you today how does it  constitute a new understanding of the  cells both on the level of the person so  individually and also socially what does  it mean for the collective where does  the control rest when you are sensing  yourself when you are creating these  feedback loops and projecting yourself  in various ways as we've seen from  Natasha's presentation today we have  very different forms and ideas of the  side work then we used to have and when  we were worried about the big  brother-type of control of surveillance  from above is today the notion of  control entirely internalized through  our system of self tracking the third  dimension of the second skin relates to  our imagination and perspective for the  future our panelists provide us with  visual imagery in material experiences  to envision possible avenues of human  evolution reproduction and the future of  the body this type of exploration  resonates with the biosuit professor  Dava Newman who works in NASA  to engineer active compressive  compression garments to allow for  increased mobility and survival in space  they also resonate with the work of  video lab professor Mary Oxman who take  such functional explorations into the  realm of art and fiction she uses  generative growth algorithms to  speculate on the future and explore how  we could redesign the relationship  between the most limited and the most  sophisticated life forms to augment our  biological functionality all the work we  have seen today interrogate the  relationship to our individual and  collective bodies by reinventing the  externalities and the internality of the  body through the wearable as an  interface between the cells and between  the collective in this new constantly  evolving space between the digital and  the material the body is linear  envisioned through flows of information  data and new sensing mechanisms in the  ways that we might develop anticipated  twenty years ago who st. Lucy's and  Cristina's work and here also the words  of Professor Skylar Tibbits expands the  material understanding of the body to  inquire pressing questions of the future  in relation to a rapidly changing  environment and new processes purposes  and ethics of Technology how do we  prepare the body to live in the 21st  century in 22nd century and 23rd century  what are the most pressing stakes for  you in your different disciplines and  practices how do we face extreme  situations ranging from climate exchange  sterilized environments life in outer  space or in culturally politically  psychologically blessed context on the  future versus thank you  [Applause]

